  "template": "Under {1}, the port serves as {2}, with the surrounding network characterized by {3}. The attribution hypothesis remains {4}; a possible primary intent is {5}. Neither conclusion is established by a single signal. The event combines {6} directed at {7}; a reported or inferred activity feature is {8}. The first indication is {9}; confidence depends on {10}, while the observed transport effect is {11}.",
  "llm": {
    "id": "clipboard",
    "url": "",
    "model": "gemma3",
    "temperature": 0.7,
    "prompts": [
      {
        "type": "narrative",
        "prompt": "Write an extensive, defensively oriented training scenario for Dutch National Police and port, rail, road, and maritime partners. Use the supplied context, activity and signals as scenario assumptions, not as evidence that an event has occurred. Develop a coherent incident at a high level, showing how an espionage, sabotage or blended event could create uncertainty across transport domains and how signals might emerge over time. Do not provide operational instructions, targeting guidance, evasion methods or tactical details. Separate observations from interpretation; do not attribute an incident to a state or group without corroboration, and identify what remains unknown. Explore plausible consequences for continuity, public confidence and cross-agency coordination, then close with questions that could support an exercise and later crime-script analysis.",
        "categories": [
          "strategic-context",
          "actor-and-intent",
          "activity-pattern",
          "signals-and-impact"
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
